<section class="purpose section-block" id="why" aria-labelledby="why-title">
  <div class="section-heading" data-reveal>
    <h2 id="why-title">What is the desktop browser for?</h2>
    <p>A real-world WAP/WML testing and exploration harness—not a modern browser in retro dress.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="purpose-body" data-reveal>
    <p class="statement">
      Waves gives old mobile-web behavior a dependable desktop place to run, inspect, and explain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Deck and card transitions, focus, forms, timers, and scripts stay in the WaveNav engine.
      Constrained fetch and WBXML work stay in Lowband and the gateway. The native host turns those
      layers into one usable session without taking their jobs away.
    </p>

    <ul class="outcome-list" aria-label="Product principles">
      <li><strong>Authentic core.</strong> Engine-owned navigation and presentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Constrained path.</strong> Transport behavior remains visible and bounded.</li>
      <li><strong>Desktop workbench.</strong> Browse, interact, recover, and inspect in one host.</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="product-capture">
      <img
        src="./waves-browser-handset.webp"
        alt="Current Waves development build showing a local WML deck in the canonical handset stage"
        width="1024"
        height="768"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Current <code>origin/main</code> capture · real WaveNav WASM path · bundled Basic Navigation deck
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
